ld-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095119 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095119 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a730ddba-a0ca-5b47-bb1a-ec0ce7519cb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096390 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096390 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x) = 12 \\cdot 2^{x},$ then $f(-4)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d03736b3-a7d8-52b3-894d-4ce2d04ee31c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096390 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate $f(-4),$ we substitute $x=-4$ into the expression for $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =12\\cdot {2}^{-4} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096391 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096391 #edb/ref 1071 "The formula $M(t)=128 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{t}$ gives the mass (in grams) of a radioactive calcium isotope after $t$ years.\nWhat mass of isotope remains after $3$ years?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a83c1301-18cf-5622-8a4a-73f9d18e36b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096391 #edb/ref 1074 "To get the mass of the isotope after $3$ years, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $M(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =128{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{t} \\\\  & =128{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\  & =128\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =128\\cdot \\frac{1}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{64} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ years, there will be $2$ grams of isotope remaining." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096392 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096392 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $C$ given that $y(t) = C \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t}$ and $y(5)=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096392 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca8cd4d6-8136-59bb-a033-6ba9ae8c06ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096392 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute $t=5$ and $y(t)=1$ into the expression for $y(t),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =C\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{5}}{{2}^{5}} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot \\frac{1}{32} \\\\ 1\\cdot 32 & =C \\\\ 32 & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=32.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096393 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096393 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(x) = 2 \\cdot 5^{3x},$ find $x$ such that $P(x)=250.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02cbb721-a880-5600-8093-fc768ae5e993" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096393 #edb/ref 1074 "First we substitute $P(x)=250$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 5^{3x}= 250\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot {5}^{3x}}{2} & =\\frac{250}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}}{\\cancel{2}} & =125 \\\\ {5}^{3x} & =125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ using the fact that $125$ is a power of $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3x} & ={5}^{3} \\\\ 3x & =3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating an Exponential Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9283940a-cad8-51b5-b8c2-dc42ad8de264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096658 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096957 #e