ow many bacteria will there be after $4$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488187 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "169115e9-c4da-5b2d-98db-fdbf90bfcfbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488187 #edb/ref 1074 "To get the number of bacteria in the culture after $4$ hours, we evaluate the function at $t=4.$ Substituting $t=4$ into the function $B(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =500\\cdot (2)^{(4)} \\\\  & =500\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =8\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $8\\,000$ bacteria in the culture after $4$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488193 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488193 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488193 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488193 #edb/ref 1071 "If $h(t)=128\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{t},$ then $h(3)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488193 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20bd28d6-3021-5539-a5f9-9ec2d307a221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488193 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate $h(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $h(t).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =128\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\  & =128\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}) \\\\  & =128\\cdot \\frac{1}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{64} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488247 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $g(t)=5\\cdot {4}^{t},$ find $t$ such that $g(t)=80.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6431deb-d4ee-50b8-b78e-d09da4574bf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488247 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we substitute $g(t)=80$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot {4}^{t}=80\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\cdot {4}^{t}}{5} & =\\frac{80}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot {4}^{t}}{\\cancel{5}} & =16 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $t$ using the fact that $16$ is a power of $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{t} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488251 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488251 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488251 #edb/ref 1071 "The population $Z(t)$ of zebras in a certain nature reserve is given by $Z(t)=10\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time in years since the reserve was opened. How many zebras will be in the reserve after $4$ years?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0a1af6d-481b-5cb9-88d9-fa115158484b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488251 #edb/ref 1074 "To get the number of zebras after $4$ years, we evaluate the function at $t=4.$ Substituting $t=4$ into the function $Z(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =10\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{(4)} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\cdot 2.073\\,6 \\\\  & =20\\,736.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, there will be $20\\,736$ zebras in the nature reserve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488261 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(t)=A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}$ and $f(3)=4,$ then $A=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c97061a8-e888-5c8e-ae09-c8d25cf5d2a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488261 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute $t=3$ and $f(t)=4$ into the expression for $f(t)$ and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot \\frac{